    <item>
        <title><![CDATA[A Hibernate Urban Legend]]></title>
        <link>http://feeds.feedburner.com/~r/techtarget/tsscom/home/~3/142807362/thread.tss</link>

        
        <description>Somewhere along the line java developers came to believe that Hibernate protects you from SQL injection. I'm not sure where they came to believe that. Maybe it is because you no longer have to write SQL and Hibernate does many other magical things - it has to protect you against SQL injection. I'm tired of telling java developers that HQL has the same vulnerabilities as SQL. You can make yourself vulnerable to SQL injection with Hibernate, too, if you avoid named parameters.
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="http://feeds.feedburner.com/~a/techtarget/tsscom/home?a=m3M78Z"&gt;&lt;img src="http://feeds.feedburner.com/~a/techtarget/tsscom/home?i=m3M78Z" border="0"&gt;&lt;/img&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;div class="feedflare"&gt;
